One thing missing from the guide and I wanted to achieve is to easily handle the case of already existing querystring parameters.

For example let's say I load https://website.com/?loggedIn=1 and I want to use the routing feature. What I'd like to do is:

```js
const search = instantsearch({
  // ... appId, apiKey...
  routing: {
    stateMapping: {
      stateToRoute(uiState) {
        return {
          loggedIn: window.isUserLoggedIn,
          q: uiState.query
        };
      },
      routeToState(routeState) {
        return {
          query: routeState.q
        };
      }
    }
  }
});
```

This ^ works and will add loggedIn query parameter to the url. But what if window.isUserLoggedIn state changes from the outside (user logs out, it's an ajax call)? I would like to turn the url to: `https://website.com/?loggedIn=0&q=phone`.

To do so for now I guess I would have to create my own router or own widget?

What I may want as a user:

```js
const search = instantsearch(...);
search.router.write(); // triggers write on the history router
```

There are implications to this so I just want to have your opinion on the proposal.
